WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.533
Redis.

2
00:00:01.833 --> 00:00:05.861
It is fast, simple, and shows up in almost every AI backend.

3
00:00:05.861 --> 00:00:06.667
Here is why.

4
00:00:07.867 --> 00:00:11.867
Redis keeps data structures in memory and serves them over the network.

5
00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:16.100
Strings, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets, and pub sub channels.

6
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.167
Each has atomic operations, and optional expiry times.

7
00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:22.667
Here is the key trick.

8
00:00:22.967 --> 00:00:26.167
Redis runs commands one at a time on a single thread.

9
00:00:26.467 --> 00:00:28.569
So a counter increment is atomic.

10
00:00:28.569 --> 00:00:32.900
Two replicas incrementing the same counter can never lose an update.

11
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:34.400
No locks needed.

12
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.133
Each structure fits a job.

13
00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:39.567
Strings with a TTL give you caching.

14
00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:42.567
Atomic counters give you quotas and rate limits.

15
00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:46.933
Sorted sets give you leaderboards, priority queues, and sliding windows.

16
00:00:47.233 --> 00:00:49.800
Lists and pub sub give you queues and events.

17
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.867
Here is a per-user daily quota.

18
00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:55.100
Each user gets one key per day.

19
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.434
We increment it.

20
00:00:56.434 --> 00:00:59.667
That is atomic, so every replica can do it safely.

21
00:00:59.967 --> 00:01:03.393
On the very first hit, we set a twenty four hour expiry.

22
00:01:03.393 --> 00:01:05.167
Then we compare to the limit.

23
00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:09.667
In the Mission Control stack, one Redis wears four hats.

24
00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:11.667
It caches semantic answers.

25
00:01:11.967 --> 00:01:14.233
It enforces per-user token quotas.

26
00:01:14.533 --> 00:01:17.333
It carries the Celery jobs for background summaries.

27
00:01:17.633 --> 00:01:21.667
And a sorted set window limits calls to the NASA DONKI API.

28
00:01:22.867 --> 00:01:25.900
In interviews, saying you used Redis is not enough.

29
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:30.344
Name the structure behind each job, and explain why INCR is atomic.

30
00:01:30.344 --> 00:01:32.200
That shows you really know it.

31
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:34.216
Let's recap.

32
00:01:34.216 --> 00:01:36.800
Redis holds data structures in memory.

33
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.600
Its single thread makes increments atomic.

34
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.867
Each structure suits a different job.

35
00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:44.727
So one Redis often plays four roles.

36
00:01:44.727 --> 00:01:46.433
Next up: message queues.
